  "query_preview": "..(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 420 AM EDT SAT OCT 24 2026\n\nThe long-term period opens Sunday evening with the stubborn 500 hPa\nridge of recent days still anchored over the region, its \npositively-tilted axis stretching from the Ark-La-Tex northeastward\nto over southern Quebec. Subsidence on the eastern side of the \nridge continues to maintain expansive surface high pressure (with\nan ~1032 mb center over the Delmarva) from the Mid/Lower \nMississippi Valley northeastward to over the Atlantic south of the\nMaritime Provinces. Well to our north, a trough is passing over \nthe Hudson Bay region, supporting an ~985 mb surface low over the\nnorthern tip of Quebec. The low's cold front trails south across \nthe province to near Ottawa and then southwest across the Upper \nGreat Lakes and Central Plains to another weaker low along the lee\nof the Colorado/New Mexico Rockies. That second surface low is \nassociated with a shortwave ejecting out of mean 500 hPa \ntroughing found along and west of the Rockies, anchored by a \nvertically stacked parent low just off the Pacific Northwest \ncoast.\n\nThe ridge aloft will flatten appreciably on Monday and Tuesday as\nthe aforementioned cold front sags southeast and eventually stalls\nnorth of the Ohio River. Meanwhile, the upstream shortwave and\nsurface low eject northeastward, pulling the boundary back to the\nnorth by Wednesday. That shortwave as well as the lingering ridge\nover the eastern CONUS will flatten and fill on Wednesday and \nThursday. A small minority of the 24/00Z LREF members maintain a \nstrong enough shortwave trough to sensibly impact the Lower Ohio \nValley. However, a vast majority of solutions maintain resilient \nridging as the 500 hPa axis shifts east of the Commonwealth, which\nwould preclude anything more than periods of passing mid and \nhigh-level cloud cover. The number of ensemble members favoring \n500 hPa troughing developing over the Ohio Valley grows on Friday,\nthough ~61/100 LREF members still cluster towards lingering\nridging. Materially, this suggests that upper-level ridging is \nstill favored to remain intact and keep dry weather in place over \neastern Kentucky through the long-term period, but forecast \nconfidence does decrease noticeably by Friday. There are still no \nrain chances in the long-term forecast at this time.\n\nAside from increased cloud cover midweek, the pattern should\ncontinue to favor fair weather with sunny, mild days, and cool,\nclear nights. Frost will be possible again in the colder sheltered\nhollows on Sunday and Monday nights. Fog will also continue to be a\nnightly phenomenon for the typical favored valley locations.\nOtherwise, look for high temperatures to range in the lower to\nmiddle 70s while sharp ridge-valley splits develop each night, with\nminimum temperatures in the mid to upper 30s in the coldest hollows\nto the mid to upper 40s on thermal belt ridges. The exception\ncould be Tuesday and Wednesday nights when more extensive cloud \ncover could keep nighttime temperatures a tad milder.",
